FUSION_ANSWER FULL_NATIVE_ALL H3 'After reviewing the three publications, the following conclusions can be drawn:\n\n1. **AI in Creative Education**:\n   - **Assistive vs. Content-Generative AI**: The study by Holmes and Tuomi (2022) and Kasneci et al. (2023) highlights the importance of distinguishing between assistive AI use (e.g., ideation, reference gathering) and content-generative AI use (e.g., producing final work). Assistive AI can support creative processes without undermining student agency, while content-generative AI can lead to process–outcome decoupling, where the visible activity of creative learning is intensified without corresponding gains in internalized competence.\n   - **Educational Outcomes**: The study suggests that AI-supported creative processes may lead to perceived support and efficiency but may not necessarily improve long-term creative development, authorship, and judgment. This is supported by the concept of cognitive offloading, where reliance on external tools can reduce the effort needed for internalization (Risko and Gilbert, 2016).\n\n2. **GenAI in Higher Education**:\n   - **Performance vs. Learning**: The review by Jose et al. (2025) and the meta-analysis by Peng et al. (2025) indicate that GenAI can enhance academic performance and creative thinking in the short term. However, these gains may be superficial, and there is a risk of cognitive erosion, where the ease of task completion may reduce the depth of learning and critical thinking.\n   - **Cognitive Paradox**: The critical thinking paradox, as framed by Jose et al. (2025), suggests that while GenAI can improve performance, it may also lead to shallower processing and reduced engagement in deep cognitive tasks. This is supported by the findings of Wang and Wu (2025) and Choudhuri et al'
