FUSION_ANSWER REVERSED_NATIVE_ALL A1 '### Causal Model to Reconcile Conflicting Results\n\n#### Evidence\n1. **Mariñoso et al. (2025)**: AI-assisted students achieved higher academic scores in designing mathematical learning situations compared to a control group using traditional resources.\n2. **Wang and Wu (2025)**: Students using AI identified gaps in depth, interpretation, and critical thinking, and engaged in critical trust and verification behaviors.\n3. **Choudhuri et al. (2026)**: Routine GenAI use was negatively associated with reflection, critical thinking, and need for understanding, especially among students with higher technophilic motivations and computer self-efficacy.\n\n#### Assumptions\n1. **Cognitive Load Theory**: AI can reduce extraneous cognitive load, potentially freeing cognitive resources for higher-level processing (Sweller et al., 2011).\n2. **Levels of Processing Framework**: Deeper processing leads to better long-term retention and transfer (Craik and Lockhart, 1972).\n3. **Desirable Difficulties**: Tasks that require more cognitive effort can lead to better long-term learning (Bjork and Bjork, 2011).\n4. **Cognitive Offloading**: Reliance on external tools can reduce the effort needed for internalization (Risko and Gilbert, 2016).\n\n#### Causal Model\n1. **Initial Performance Gain**:\n   - **Mechanism**: AI reduces extraneous cognitive load, making tasks easier and more efficient.\n   - **Prediction**: Students using AI will show higher initial performance on tasks (Mariñoso et al., 2025).\n\n2. **Surface-Level Processing**:\n   - **Mechanism**: The ease provided by AI can lead to surface-level processing, where students rely on AI-generated content without deep cognitive engagement.'
