FUSION_ANSWER REVERSED_NATIVE_ALL A3 'The most consequential unresolved contradiction in the literature on generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) in education is the **critical thinking paradox**: the observation that while GenAI can enhance performance and efficiency, it may simultaneously erode deeper cognitive processes such as critical thinking, reflection, and long-term retention.\n\n### Rival Explanations\n\n1. **Surface-Level Processing Hypothesis**:\n   - **Explanation**: GenAI tools provide ready-made answers and solutions, which can lead to surface-level processing. Students may rely on these tools to bypass the cognitive struggle necessary for deep conceptual understanding and critical thinking.\n   - **Implication**: If this hypothesis is correct, the use of GenAI will lead to higher short-term performance but may result in poorer long-term retention and transfer of knowledge.\n\n2. **Cognitive Offloading Hypothesis**:\n   - **Explanation**: Students may offload cognitive tasks to GenAI, reducing the mental effort required to complete tasks. This offloading can lead to a reduction in the development of internal cognitive skills, such as critical thinking and problem-solving.\n   - **Implication**: If this hypothesis is correct, the use of GenAI will lead to higher short-term performance but may result in a lack of development of deeper cognitive skills, which are crucial for long-term learning and problem-solving.\n\n### Smallest Decisive Test\n\n**Design**:\n- **Participants**: A group of undergraduate students from a variety of disciplines.\n- **Conditions**:\n  - **Control Group**: Students complete a set of complex problem-solving tasks without the use of GenAI.\n  - **Experimental Group**: Students complete the same set of tasks with the assistance of GenAI.\n- **Measures**:\n  - **Short-Term Performance**: Immediate performance on the problem-solving tasks.\n  - **Long-Term Retention**: Performance on a delayed test of the same or similar tasks, administered one month'
